{
 "lead": "Absorption is not isothermal. Dissolving gas releases heat — heat of solution, often latent heat too. Warm solvent holds less gas, so the equilibrium line climbs toward the operating line and the driving force shrinks. Cold-data design underperforms. Amine sweetening, HCl absorption and SO2 scrubbing all feel this.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#gas-solubility-in-liquids-and-henrys-law\">Henry's law</a>, <a href=\"#equilibrium-and-operating-lines-in-absorption\">the Y–X plot</a> and <a href=\"#htu-ntu-and-design-of-packed-absorbers\">HTU/NTU</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Where the heat comes from",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Three sources add up. Heat of solution ΔH_s is released when solute enters the solvent; large for HCl in water, small for CO2. Latent heat of condensation adds for condensable vapours. Heat of dilution appears as the rich solution concentrates down the tower."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "ΔT = (G_s ΔH_s) / (L c_p)",
     "where": "ΔT is the adiabatic liquid temperature rise in K; G_s the solute absorbed in kmol/h; ΔH_s the heat released per kmol solute in kJ/kmol; L the liquid flow in kmol/h; c_p the liquid heat capacity in kJ/(kmol·K). For mass units use kg/h and kJ/(kg·K)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Mass form: ΔT = (G_s ΔH_s)/(ṁ c_p), with ṁ in kg/h and c_p in kJ/(kg·K). The formula assumes all heat stays in the liquid. Real towers lose some through the wall, so measured rise is lower — treat ΔT as worst case."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "What warmth does to the two lines",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Henry constant H rises with temperature, so m = H/P rises. For SO2 in water m grows about 4 % per kelvin. On the Y–X plot the equilibrium line swings upward while the operating line stays put. The driving force narrows at the bottom, where liquid is richest and hottest, and can close."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "On Y–X coordinates the operating line uses solute-free flows L_s and G_s, which stay constant, so the line is straight even for a concentrated tower. Curvature appears only if you plot mole or mass fractions with total flows, or if solvent condenses or evaporates. A large temperature swing curves the equilibrium line, not the operating line."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Designing for it, and stripping",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Two routes work. Step stages graphically, reading the equilibrium line at each stage's local liquid temperature and carrying the heat balance forward. Or integrate packed height with an energy balance alongside the mass balance, updating m at every height. Both need solubility data at several temperatures."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Practical fixes are cheaper than a taller tower:"
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "Send more solvent: L rises, ΔT falls, and the operating line moves away from equilibrium.",
      "Intercool the liquid between packed sections, or split the tower with a cooler between.",
      "Feed the solvent cold, using its sensible heat capacity to soak up the heat of solution."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Stripping is the mirror image. It takes heat in — heat of desorption plus sensible heat — from a reboiler or live steam. Part of that heat warms the liquid; the rising m pushes the equilibrium line up, away from the operating line (which lies below it), so the driving force grows. If the tower ran cold, m would fall, the equilibrium line would drop towards the operating line, and the driving force would shrink. Amine regeneration is the standard case."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
